 - **Optional first economic unit before the capital's first defender:** when the first and only capital is not in danger, it can produce one immediately useful Work Boat or required land Worker before filling an empty city garrison. This should be more efficient because it avoids delaying the first improvement and the growth, production, and resources it unlocks, and animals are not a serious city-capture threat. However, because a human can try an unusually early rush against the temporarily empty capital, this tradeoff is XML-tunable and can be disabled to restore Base AdvCiv's defender-first behavior. This is as of now only applicable to the first city, so the unchanged no-defender priority resumes after this first economic unit.
 - **Prioritize useful Work Boats for seafood before land Workers:** after producing an immediate defender when needed, a safe AI city now builds a Work Boat before its land Worker when it has seafood that can be improved now (unless immediate defense and danger/Turtle safeguards take precedence). This is more efficient because improving seafood increases food without stopping city growth during production, and this extra food helps the following Worker and later production be produced faster. Note: the AI does not waste a Work Boat on unavailable improvements such as Whale before the required technology like as of now `TECH_SEAFARING` is discovered. Note: also remove the Base AdvCiv population-3 cutoff that stopped Work Boat production midway (See [KI#174](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#174---fixedimproved-base-advciv-work-boat-icitypopulation--3-cutoff-in-cvcityaiai_chooseproduction-interrupted-seafood-production-for-a-land-worker)). After these changes, in save file 443 BBAI tests, Seoul successfully selected a Work Boat for its available Molluscs on turn 5 instead of delaying it until about turn 17 (to produce a worker before it), while Mutal correctly built its Worker first because Maya lacked the as of now required `TECH_FISHING` for Molluscs and Fish, then selected a Work Boat on turn 12 when this tech was discovered and thus these bonuses became improvable.
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6060+) **Isolated cities no longer produce land civilian units that cannot perform their role:** for example, in save file 450, BBAI logging showed the one-tile island city Bactra producing a Worker that remained idle because none of its plots could be improved; the same could happen, for example, on a landmass containing only plots the AI cannot improve. To fix this, the Worker-demand calculation no longer creates a minimum need when no local plot can be improved, and the AI now also refuses a Worker when its landmass already has enough, as well as land Explorer units when no exploration remains there, and land Spy units in a secondary isolated colony with neither a known rival city nor an adjacent Spy transport. Primary landmasses remain eligible so an overseas empire can still produce Spies and their transports. Note: this check is central in `CvCityAI::AI_chooseUnit` to make sure no short-circuit or unexpected logic bypasses it, as with AdvCiv-SAS's central unit-scrapping safeguards. See [KI#192](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#192---fixedimproved-base-advcivk-mod-island-worker-logistics-and-advciv-sas-production-safety-gap-could-strand-workers-and-other-land-civilians-where-they-had-no-use).
 - **Emergency water-food / water-hammer buildings (pre-fallback):** Before the "no-production" fallback runs, if a **coastal (ocean-connected)** city is **low food per turn**, force a **water-food building class** (e.g., **Harbor** or civ variants that give +1 food on water tiles) to jump-start growth. Also force the **water-hammer building class** (e.g., **Port**) for **high-enough pop** (enough headcount so it is worth investing on water plots) coastal cities unless they are very good and don't need it: cities that **still have fairly low production** or **not too high food surplus**; Port can take significant time, but these cities are **unlikely to contribute much short-term production anyway**, and hammer on water tiles makes their later infrastructure and future building time **more helpful later on**. Immediate danger / defense logic still runs earlier, so Walls/Castle can still go first when needed, otherwise the **risk is deemed worth the delay** to produce these buildings. Runs in `CvCity::doTurn`.
 - **Emergency defense priority (risk-aware):** When invasion risk is high—enemy team **significantly stronger**, **nearby danger** detected, or **at war** with an opponent not significantly weaker—**force-build** **Walls**, then **Castle** (if missing) at top priority. Triggers are conservative to avoid growth drag. Implemented in `CvCity::AI_doTurn`. Early autoplay suggests fewer snowball steamrolls. **Update:** Skip this emergency defense path for **very low-hammer** cities (scaled by era) or cities whose inner BFC is **mostly water tiles**, as these are better used as economy cities and unlikely to hold the main front; this also prevents triggering fallback-unit / emergency-defense builds too early and avoids stalling early growth. **Update 2:** For **water-heavy cities**, this emergency-defense path is **disabled**; see the related Update 2 note below.
 - **"No production" stall — worked around / fixed**: AI cities no longer sit on **no production**. Added a robust fallback: produce the **most expensive suitable land combat unit**; if a candidate is **extremely overpriced for the era**, switch to the **cheapest suitable** instead. **Civilians** (e.g., scouts) are excluded. This prevents idle turns (e.g., will build an archer in the Ancient era) and cuts wasted hammers. Added in `CvCity::AI_doTurn`. See [KI#51](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#51---worked-around--fixed-massive-seemingly-base-advciv---civ4-issue-if-im-not-mistaken-of-many-cities-entering-no-production-early-for-1-or-several-turns-many-times-during-the-game-early-and-possibly-later-this-is-why-many-cities-have-a-process-rather-than-no-production-as-processes-are-not-available-early-and-are-listed-among-fallbacks-if-production-fails-it-seems-but-check-to-be-sure). Related: handicap settings were **slightly reduced** to keep difficulty even with the new efficiency. **Update:** Added **offense-only** and **defense-only** selection modes inside this fallback (auto-reverting to "any suitable land unit" if no candidates exist). This is intended to avoid bypassing `CvCityAI::AI_chooseUnit` limits and to reduce excessive **Longbowmen** when the fallback triggers. **Update 2:** For **water-heavy cities**, when the **no-production fallback** in `CvCity::AI_doTurn` triggers, try to use key **water-focused economic buildings** (food / hammers / gold) as the fallback instead of a generic land-unit fallback; this is usually more efficient for growth/economy, and such cities are less likely to be main land-attack targets. See also [Buildings](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md#buildings) or Sevopedia/XML.
@@ -357,6 +358,7 @@ Note 2: Master–vassal(s) changes are intended to strengthen overall AI play by
 - Always improve **bonuses first**, prioritizing **food** bonuses. Major refactor of `CvUnitAI::AI_bestCityBuild` to use a probabilistic value tree tuned for AdvCiv-SAS. See [KI#30](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#30---attemptingly-fixed-ai-workers-not-prioritizing-bonuses-to-improve-especially-food-bonuses-and-other-ai-workers-optimizations-orand-changes).
 - One worker per improvement **cap** to avoid over-stacking on slow tiles and reduce capture risk.
 - **Reliable Worker availability without early overproduction:** AI cities rebuild enough Workers for each land area before other production priorities can leave them short indefinitely, without producing more than the AI calculates it needs. Maintaining enough Workers throughout the game lets the AI improve food/production/commerce, connect resources and routes, repair pillaging, and develop new or underdeveloped cities sooner, especially after Worker losses or earlier underproduction. This normally aims for 1 Worker per city plus a capital-area reserve. As a refinement, a one-city empire needs at most 1 land Worker until its capital has ever reached a tunable population (5 by default), avoiding early double-Worker production and the resulting halted growth; normal demand resumes after the capital matures or another city is founded. Ordinary Worker choices, cases where a blocked Settler choice falls back to a Worker, general unit selection, and Worker scrapping now use the same centralized target. Four BBAI test runs then completed exactly 1 early Worker per empire, with the only later automatic second Worker beginning when its capital reached population 5.
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6060+) **Workers are no longer delivered to landmasses that already have enough:** in save file 450, BBAI logging showed a Worker accompanying the Settler to the island where Thapsus was founded, then remaining idle there for 93 turns because none of its plots could be improved. Our first attempt at this fix correctly left an unnecessary Worker on the large landmass (i.e. home) instead of sending it to the small unimprovable island, but exposed a separate problem: general Worker logic kept loading the Worker back onto the island-bound Settler transport, so our early fix had to unload the same Worker back onto the large landmass 14 times. After our second version correctly stopped that loop, testing exposed a third failure: a separate Worker-ferry routine still kept sending extra Workers to islands that already had enough, eventually crowding 5 Workers onto Sardis's two-tile landmass and 2 onto Ergili's three-tile landmass, where most had no useful work. To fix this, Settler transports now leave unnecessary Workers on their origin landmass or keep them aboard for another destination; Workers cannot repeatedly reboard the same transport after being left behind, and later Worker ferries now stop once the destination landmass has enough. These checks use current need and current Worker count, so delivery becomes eligible again if losses leave the landmass short or new work requires workers again. In the confirming run, the AI correctly left a Worker on its origin landmass because the destination needed 1 but already had 3. See [KI#192](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#192---fixedimproved-base-advcivk-mod-island-worker-logistics-and-advciv-sas-production-safety-gap-could-strand-workers-and-other-land-civilians-where-they-had-no-use).
 - **Yield-first build execution:** workers build the exact improvement selected through the full city/plot evaluation instead of letting the older `AI_betterPlotBuild` postprocessing replace a scored Chop, Mine, Farm, etc. with an unrelated Road. Roads/routes remain strongly de-prioritized early unless they are truly the best move, while important connectable bonuses are still connected. See [KI#31](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#31---attemptingly-fixed-deprioritize-routes-for-ai-workers-in-favour-of-yields-first-except-in-some-rare-cases).
 - **Overhaul: `AI_bestCityBuild` (full routine):** "holy" improvements (never overwrite: **hamlet, village, town, workshop**) and "semi-holy" (contextual: **farms** in food-poor cities, **cottages** in food-rich cities); fast/ordered improvement; **bonus-specific rules** (e.g., don’t cottage bananas; prefer plantation or a farm only if it makes clear sense); **terrain-aware cottage/farm logic** (flood plains favored for cottages unless the city is food-poor); **minimal oscillation**; prefer steady growth; avoid overwriting until other workable tiles are improved; hold top tiles for later if the "now" move would be inferior; prefer farms on low-food BFCs. See [KI#33](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#33---tremendously-improved-and-fixed-and-enhanced-ai-worker-buildimprovement-logic-in-cvunitaiai_bestcitybuild). Update: worker AI now also weighs the **longer-term value** of strong **non-bonus farms** for **low-food or hill-heavy cities**: they can **support food-consuming mines**, **faster growth**, **more aggressive early slaving**, or **specialist economies**; concrete dry-farm irrigation-chain fixes can replace the least harmful blocker first; and the logic is more **mindful of preserving useful irrigation paths** instead of blindly maximizing immediate cottage/workshop yield (**Reserve** likely irrigation/farm connector plots **from non-farm builds** to reduce AI worker farm/non-farm oscillation).
 - **Worker lower-food replacement guard:** from a **tunable era onward** (default as of now: Medieval), AI workers no longer replace existing **no-bonus** improvements with lower-food alternatives. This targets **repeated food-threshold oscillation** such as **Farm → Cottage → Farm → Cottage** or **Mine → Windmill → Mine** after the city briefly looks food-safe. It still allows equal-food retools, food-restoration changes such as Cottage → Farm, and cleanup of City Ruins or obsolete/non-active improvements. In the tested BBAI log, the worst Mali farm/cottage loops reached Farm again and then skipped later Farm → Cottage attempts from about T128/T130 onward. This change is because before this era, cities are often small or health/happiness-capped (extra/excess food may not necessarily help or could be inefficient early) and early cottages still have time to mature, but later, cities are usually larger, health sources are generally multiple, and specialist yields are stronger than plot yields (so any food can be used and is generally strong/stronger), and cottages/growth improvements have less time to grow and pay back, so preserving food support is usually stronger.

